== A Husband’s Humble Gentleness to His Wife

=== §. 13. #emph[Of a husband’s kind acceptance of the things his wife does.]#footnote(numbering: (..) => "*")[Treatise 3, §. 10.]

Saint Peter gives a general rule for a husband’s outward conduct toward his wife: that he dwell with her #emph[according to knowledge,] that is, as a man able to order his conduct wisely, to his own honor and his wife’s good, so that she may have just cause to bless God that she was ever joined to such a husband.

Out of this general rule these two branches sprout.

+ That a husband give no just offense to his wife.
+ That he wisely deal with any offense given by her.

To avoid giving offense he must have regard

+ To what she does as duty to him.
+ To what he does as duty to her.

With regard to the former, two things are required:

+ That he kindly accept what she is willing and able to do.
+ That he wisely commend and reward what she does well.

Having thus laid down these heads for order’s sake, I will handle the separate points one by one.

The first particular in which a husband shows himself #footnote[VI. Husbands must kindly accept every good duty of their wives. Gen 21:8.] to be a man of knowledge in walking before his wife is #emph[a kind and respectful acceptance of every good duty his wife performs.] Abraham, as a testimony of his kind acceptance of Sarah’s pains in nursing her child, held a great feast when the child was weaned; and Elkanah, with a like regard, gave his wife liberty #footnote[1 Sam 1:23.] to do what seemed best to her.

This must be a great encouragement to wives to be subject to their husbands in all things, when they see that no part of their subjection is carelessly neglected, but rather graciously accepted. It puts life into a wife’s spirit to think that her care and pains in pleasing her husband will not be in vain.

=== §. 14. #emph[Of husbands’ slighting and rejecting their wives’ goodness.]

Contrary is the practice of those who, thinking all that a wife #footnote[Contrary, 1. To take no notice of their wives’ goodness.] does to be only her duty, take little or no notice of it; or, if they cannot help noticing it, yet regard it lightly and pass over it carelessly. This often makes a wife even regret the good she has done, as David regretted the service #footnote[2\. Carelessly to pass it over. 1 Sam 25:21.] he had done for Nabal. The truth is that wives ought to look to God for his acceptance rather than to their husbands; and though their husbands will take no notice of the good things they do, or not regard them, yet they ought to do their duty for conscience’ sake and for the Lord’s sake. But even so, considering our weakness and reluctance toward every duty, it cannot be denied that a husband’s slight regard for his wife’s goodness gives her occasion to grow weary of it, and that he does as much as lies in him to make her regret it.

But what may we say of those who scornfully reject their wives’ #footnote[3\. Scornfully to reject it.] duty, and indeed like them the worse for making conscience of it, and so (quite contrary to the rule of Christianity) “overcome good with evil”?#footnote[Rom 12:21.] Surely they show a very diabolical spirit in themselves, and cannot help causing their wives much grief and offense, and making what their wives do very irksome and tedious. Fathers ought not to provoke their #footnote[Eph 6:4.] children, much less husbands their wives.

=== §. 15. #emph[Of husbands’ courteous acceptance of their wives’ reverent bearing.]#footnote(numbering: (..) => "*")[Treatise 3, §. 11. 55.]

For a better understanding of so needful a point, I will #footnote[How a man’s kind acceptance of his wife’s duty is shown.] apply it somewhat more particularly and distinctly to the separate duties of a wife, which were drawn under two heads:

#emph[Reverence.]

#emph[Obedience.]

For the first: if a wife shows her dutiful respect for her #footnote[1\. By answering reverence with courtesy. #emph[Depone asperitatem morum cum tibi sedula vxor occurrit, etc. Ambr. Hexaem. lib.] 5. #emph[cap.] 7. \[Lay aside harshness of manner when your attentive wife comes to meet you, etc.\]] husband by any reverent behavior, gesture, or speech, he ought to meet her (as we say) halfway, and show his gracious acceptance of it by some like courteous behavior, gesture, and speech, seemly, not foolish.

#emph[Objection.] In this way a husband will abase himself and disgrace his place.

#emph[Answer.] The courtesy I speak of, as it comes from a superior, being a purely voluntary matter and a token #footnote[Courtesy can stand with superiority.] of kindness and favor, is no abasement of himself, but an advancement of his inferior: a great grace to her, no disgrace to him. Abram was counted by the Hittites “a prince of #footnote[Gen 23:6, 7.] God,” yet in speaking with them “he bowed down” to them. It is noted as a commendable thing in Esau that, though he was at that time his brother’s superior (at least he took himself to be so), yet, seeing how Jacob reverenced him, bowing seven times to the ground, he “ran to him and embraced #footnote[Gen 33:3, 4.] him, threw his arms around his neck.” Most to the point is the example of King Ahasuerus, who, seeing Esther standing reverently #footnote[Esth 5:2.] before him, held out his scepter to her, which in a king is great courtesy.

But to put the matter beyond all question, let the example of Christ noted in Solomon’s Song be observed, and we will find his courtesy in every way answering the reverence of his spouse.

=== §. 15. #emph[Of husbands’ too great loftiness.]

Contrary is a lofty bearing of husbands toward their wives, who pay no regard to all the reverence their wives show, regarding their wives in this no more than children or servants, or no more than kings regard the reverence of their subjects.

I have often noted that there is a great difference between a wife and all other inferiors, and in that respect every sign of her reverence should be much better regarded. Yet we know that kings and queens will put out their hands to be kissed by their subjects when they kneel before them, which is a token of courtesy: how much more ought husbands to show courtesy! They are unworthy to be reverenced by their wives who, too much like lords, look down on them.

=== §. 16. #emph[Of husbands’ ready yielding to their wives’ humble requests.]#footnote(numbering: (..) => "*")[Treatise 3, §. 15.]

Again, since it is a token of reverence in a wife humbly to #footnote[2\. By granting her humble request.] make her desire known to her husband, he ought to show so much courtesy as to grant her desire readily. This courtesy Ahasuerus, named before,#footnote[Esth 5:3.] showed to Esther; David#footnote[1 Kgs 1:28. #emph[etc.]] to Bathsheba; Isaac#footnote[Gen 28:1.] to Rebekah; Abraham#footnote[Gen 16:6.] to Sarah; and many other husbands to their wives. Abraham#footnote[Gen 21:10, 11.] showed such respect for his wife in this that, though the thing she desired was grievous to him, yet he yielded to his wife.

#emph[Objection.] God first commanded him to do so.

#emph[Answer.] This adds the more force to the argument, showing that it is God’s express will that a husband should show this kind of courtesy to his wife. A man ought to do much more at his wife’s request than at anyone else’s, whether friend, child, or parent; indeed, he ought to show himself much more free, eager and cheerful in granting his wife’s request than anyone else’s: provided, however, that what she desires is something that may lawfully be granted. To yield in unlawful things is to lose his authority, as was shown #footnote[§. 5.] before.

=== §. 17. #emph[Of husbands’ harshness to their wives.]

Contrary is the harshness of the disposition of those who yield to their wives’ request as a hard-milking cow lets down her milk, not without much ado; and by this all the grace of their yielding is taken away. There can be no courtesy in yielding when it is forced from them against their mind and will. Their wives must ask and plead again and again, indeed be forced to use the mediation of others to persuade their husbands to yield to their request, before they will yield, if they yield at all. What is this but to proclaim to all the world that they have no affection for their wives? If a wife’s breath is alien to her husband, surely his heart is first estranged from her; and that is the ready way to make him set his heart on forbidden women.

=== §. 18. #emph[Of husbands’ forbearing to demand all that they may.]#footnote(numbering: (..) => "*")[Treatise 3, §. 38, 39, #emph[etc.] and §. 43, 44, #emph[etc.]]

As a wife’s #emph[reverence,] so also her #emph[obedience] must be answered with her husband’s courtesy. As evidence of this, #emph[a husband] #footnote[3\. By accepting what she is willing to do.] #emph[must be ready to accept that in which his wife shows herself willing to obey him.] He ought to be sparing in demanding too much of her. In this he ought so to frame his conduct toward her that the obedience she performs may come from her own voluntary disposition, from a free conscience toward God (even because God has placed her in a place of subjection), and from a wifely love, rather than from any demand on her husband’s part, and as it were by force.

Husbands ought not to demand of their wives everything #footnote[4\. By forbearing to demand too much.] that wives ought to yield to if it is demanded. They must consider what is lawful, needful, fitting, advisable and suitable for their wives to do, indeed, what they are most willing to do, before they are too peremptory in demanding it. For example:

1\. Though the wife ought to go with her husband and #footnote[5\. By allowing her to live where it is most fitting for her.] live where he thinks fit, yet he ought not (unless he is forced to it by some urgent calling) to move her from place to place, and carry her away from a place where she is well settled, without her approval. Jacob#footnote[Gen 31:4, #emph[etc.]] consulted his wives and found out whether they were willing before he carried them away from their father’s house.

2\. Though she ought cheerfully to receive whatever guests #footnote[6\. By forbearing to tire her with guests.] he brings into the house, yet he ought not to be grievous and burdensome to her in this. The greatest care and pains in receiving guests fall on the wife; she ought therefore to be treated tenderly in it.

If he sees that she is conscientious and wise, well able to manage #footnote[7\. By allowing her to manage the affairs of the house.] and order matters about the house, yet unwilling to do anything without his consent, he ought to be ready and free in giving his consent and satisfying her desire, as Elkanah was;#footnote[1 Sam 1:23.] and if she is shy and reluctant to ask consent, he ought to offer it of his own accord. Indeed, he ought to give her a general consent to order and arrange matters as in her wisdom she sees fit, as the same Elkanah did (“Do,” he says to his wife, “what you think is best”), and as #footnote[Prov 31:11.] the husband of that good housewife whom Solomon describes did.

A general consent is especially needed for ordering household affairs. For it is a charge laid on wives to #footnote[1 Tim 5:14 οἰκοδεσποτεῖν.] “govern their households”; by which it appears that the business of the house belongs to the wife, and is most especially hers. In this respect she is called the #footnote[Materfamilias.] #emph[housewife.] So in these matters husbands ought to leave things to their ordering, and not restrain them in every particular from doing anything without special leave and direction. To illustrate this in #footnote[What things are to be left to the wife’s ordering.] some particulars, it belongs particularly to a wife:

+ To #footnote[Prov 31:21, 22.] order the decorating and adorning of the house.
+ To #footnote[#emph[v.] 15.] arrange the ordinary provisions for the family.
+ To #footnote[Gen 16:6.] rule and govern the maidservants.
+ To #footnote[1 Tim 5:10. Titus 2:4.] bring up the children while they are young, and the like.

He ought therefore, with a general consent, to leave these to her discretion, limited only by these two #footnote[2 Kgs 4:19.] cautions.

1\. That she has in some measure sufficient discretion, #footnote[Cautions about leaving matters of the house to the wife’s ordering.] understanding and wisdom, and is not too ignorant, foolish, simple, wasteful, etc.

2\. That he keeps a general oversight of all, and so interposes his authority that he allows nothing unlawful or unseemly to be done by his wife about the house, children, servants, or other things: for

#[
#set enum(indent: 24pt)
+ The general charge of all lies principally on him.
+ He will give an account to God for all things that are wrong in his house.
+ The blame for all will also lie on him before men.
]

But with those two cautions provided, he ought, together with his general consent, to put #emph[trust]#footnote[Prov 31:11.] in his wife (as Potiphar#footnote[Gen 39:6. Difference between the trust put in a wife and in others.] did in Joseph), making in this a difference between a wife and all others, whether grown children, friends, or servants, whom he employs in his affairs. He may direct them in every particular, for matter and manner, and take a strict account of them for expenses laid out or other things done, because what they do is wholly and only for another. To his wife (who is a joint parent of his children and governor of his house, to whose good the husband’s wealth comes, and who in that respect does for herself what she does for her husband) greater liberty and freedom must be given.

=== §. 19. #emph[Of husbands’ too great strictness toward their wives.]

Contrary is the harshness and severity of many husbands who stand on the utmost step of their authority and yield no more to a wife than to any other inferior. Such are those

1\. Who are never content or satisfied with any duty the #footnote[In what things a husband shows too much strictness.] wife performs, but are always demanding more and more.

2\. Who do not care how #emph[grievous] and #emph[burdensome] they are to their wives: #emph[grievous,] by bringing into the house guests they know cannot be welcome to them; #emph[burdensome,] by #emph[too frequent] and #emph[unseasonable] invitations of guests, or by imposing other such extraordinary business, over and above the ordinary affairs of the house. #emph[Too frequent] imposing of such things cannot help breeding much weariness. #emph[Unseasonable] imposing (as when the wife is weak through sickness, childbearing, nursing, or other such causes, and so not able to give the satisfaction she otherwise would) cannot help greatly disquieting her and giving her great offense.

3\. Who hold their wives under as if they were children or servants, restraining them from doing anything without their knowledge and particular, express consent.

4\. Who are overbusy in prying into every business of the house, and will have their hand in everything. Besides giving their wives no opportunity to show the understanding, intelligence, wisdom, care, and other gifts with which God has endowed them, such husbands take away the main end for which a wife was given to a man, namely, to be “a helper.”#footnote[Gen 2:18.] Such husbands cannot help neglecting other weightier matters which belong more properly to them. For observe it and you#super[+] will find that the husbands who are busiest about the private affairs of the house that belong to their wives are the most negligent of the affairs that belong to themselves. They think they #emph[walk in integrity,] but they are not #emph[just] or #emph[wise] in this. For “the just man walks in HIS integrity,” #footnote[Prov 20:7; 14:8.] and “the wisdom of the prudent is to discern HIS way”: that is, the integrity which belongs to his own particular place, and his own way. But “every fool will be meddling,” namely, with things not belonging to his place. #footnote[Prov 20:3.]

5\. Who are oversuspicious of their wives, and therefore overstrict in calling them to account. Saint Paul calls suspicions #footnote[1 Tim 6:4.] “evil,” and not without just cause: for they are evil in their #emph[nature,] and evil in their #emph[effects,] being the occasion of much harm; but in none are they so evil as in husbands toward their wives. If a wife’s faithfulness is suspected without just cause (and the welfare of the family and the increase of its wealth come to her good as well as to the husband’s), who shall be trusted? It is the ruin of many families that servants are trusted, and not wives.

So much for a husband’s kind acceptance of what his wife is willing and able to do.

=== §. 20. #emph[Of husbands’ encouraging their wives in good things.]

The #emph[love] which a husband owes to his wife further requires #footnote[Husbands must commend and reward good things in their wives.] that he wisely #emph[commend] and #emph[reward] what she has #emph[done well.] What the apostle says of the magistrate’s authority may fitly be applied to a husband’s, in relation to his wife: “Do what is good, and you will have his praise.”#footnote[Rom 13:3.] It is expressly noted in the description of a good husband that “he praises” his wife;#footnote[Prov 31:28, 29.] and when it says, “Give her #footnote[#emph[v.] 31.] the fruit of her hands,” it is implied also that he rewards her.

This is an undoubted evidence of his kind acceptance of her duty, and a further encouragement to stir her up to go on and continue in well-doing. Indeed, it is also an evidence of his joy and delight both in her person and in her well-doing. If there is no delight in someone’s person, his well-doing will stir up envy rather than joy; and those who envy a man’s well-doing will never commend or reward him for it.

In a husband’s commending of his wife this caveat must be added: that he so orders his praise that it does not smack of flattery or foolish doting, nor #footnote[#emph[Lege apud T. Liv. Dec.] 1. #emph[l.] 1. #emph[quid inde evenit, quòd regij iuuenes suam quisque vxorem miris modis laudare.] \[Read in Livy what came of it, that the king’s young sons each praised his own wife in wonderful terms.\]] stir up lust or envy in others.

=== §. 21. #emph[Of husbands’ ungrateful discouraging of their wives.]

Contrary is the ungrateful, if not envious, disposition of husbands #footnote[#emph[Quisque suam laudat, studijs certamina crescunt. Ouid. Fast. lib.] 2. \[Each praises his own; with their eagerness the contest grows.\]] who pass by many good things that their wives ordinarily do every day, without any approval, commendation or reward, yet are ready to find fault with the least slip or neglect in them; and that in such general terms, as if they never did anything well, so that their wives may well complain and say, as it is in the proverb,

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[#emph[Oft did I well, and that hear I never:]]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[#emph[Once did I ill, and that hear I ever.]]

Yet such men will be ready to praise other men’s wives, and to reproach their own wives with the examples of those others, when their own far excel them in every kind of goodness. What does this show but that either they take no notice of their own wives’ goodness, or else regard it little because it is so common? If their wives do not have the more grace in them, this disposition is enough not only to discourage them from doing any good duty, but also to breed jealousy in them, and to turn their hearts away from their husbands.

=== §. 22. #emph[Of a husband’s mildness.]#footnote(numbering: (..) => "*")[Treatise 3, §. 10.]

So much for the regard a husband is to have for the duty his wife performs to him.

To avoid giving just offense, a husband must further have #emph[good regard for what he does as duty to his wife.] As he must accept duty #emph[kindly] at his wife’s hands, so he must #emph[mildly] perform the #footnote[Oportet virum praeesse vxori oblectando, & indulgendo. Plut. praec. Connub. \[A husband ought to rule over his wife by delighting her and indulging her.\]] duty he owes to her.

This mildness is a special fruit and evidence of love, and a notable means of taking away all the offense that might otherwise be taken at many things he does. Sugar and honey are not more pleasant to the tongue than mildness is to the heart. It causes things that are otherwise irksome and grievous to the soul to be well received and taken in, just as bitter pills dipped in sweet syrup, or rolled up in the soft pulp of an apple, are soon swallowed down and well digested. If a husband desires to be counted a servant of the Lord, he must learn this lesson: for “the servant of the Lord must … be gentle to all men.”#footnote[2 Tim 2:24.] If any other servant of the Lord must, much more husbands; if to #emph[all men,] most of all to their wives; and that in many respects.

+ Because of the close union between man and wife.
+ Because of the joint authority she has with him over others: so that in this he may be a precedent and example to her.
+ Because of her weakness: glasses are handled tenderly; a small knock soon breaks them.

=== §. 23. #emph[Of husbands’ bitterness.]

Contrary is #emph[bitterness,] a vice expressly forbidden, and that to husbands in particular. It is a vice that cannot stand with a husbandly love; and so the apostle, commanding the one, forbids the other: “Love,” he says, “and do not be bitter.”#footnote[Col 3:19.] Nothing more blunts the edge of his authority, perverts the use of his government, provokes his wife’s resentment, or makes his words and deeds less regarded, than #emph[bitterness.] It is like gall and wormwood mixed with sweet and wholesome foods, which keeps them from being well digested, so that they are violently spat out again as soon as ever they are tasted. Men in authority are very prone to this; and therefore, husbands, be all the more watchful against it: “love your#super[+] wives and do not be bitter toward them.”
